• Implemented the float64 lowering path and got dfsin onto real hardware simulation.

  The main fixes are in builder.go, call_inline.go, emitter.go, main.go, and sim_internal_test.go. Native float64 binops/unops/conversions now lower through soft-float
  support, math.Float64bits and math.Abs are handled, float constants are emitted as IEEE bit patterns, and countLeadingZeros32/64 are synthesized instead of depending
  on the local lookup-table array. I also had to patch native +/- lowering to avoid the buggy float64_add wrapper and dispatch directly through addFloat64Sigs/
  subFloat64Sigs with sign-aware operand ordering.

  Verification:
  go test ./cmd/mygo -count=1 passed.
  go test ./internal/ir -count=1 passed.
  go run ./cmd/mygo sim --sim-max-cycles 200000 --expect tests/CHStone/dfsin/software.stdout.txt tests/CHStone/dfsin/main.go passed.
  The run used real Verilator; stderr included make: Entering directory ....

  I did not regenerate tests/CHStone/dfsin/simulation.result.txt, so any checked-in hardware_cycles_used=1 there is still stale. The direct sim run is now definitely
  hardware-backed rather than the old software fallback.


